+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` in `src/patch.ts` already has private `clone`, `equal`, `isObject`, `has` helpers. Inputs come from `JSON.parse`, so own members like `__proto__` are possible.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** a correct (round-trips through `applyPatch`) and compact patch, recursing into objects and arrays.
+
+**Non-Goals:** `move`/`copy` detection, `test` operations, an optimal (LCS/edit-distance) array diff.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Recursive diff: equal → nothing; both objects → per-key `remove` / `add` / recurse; both arrays → array diff; otherwise `replace` at the path.
+- Arrays: trim the common prefix and suffix (so a front insertion is one `add`), then pair the remaining middle elements by index and recurse, `replace`-ing/recursing for paired ones, then `remove` surplus old elements from highest index down, or `add` surplus new ones in ascending order. Simple, correct, and linear; not always the shortest for exotic reorderings.
+- Operations are emitted so each path is valid at the time it applies (removals descending, adds ascending).
+- Values placed in the patch are deep-cloned; `clone`, `equal` are exported from `patch.ts` for reuse (own-property-safe).
+- Pointer tokens are escaped with `~0`/`~1`.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+Index-wise middle diff can produce more operations than LCS for shuffles; accepted for simplicity.
